1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:01,925
A runner on a track.

2
00:00:01,925 --> 00:00:03,209
A bus on a road.

3
00:00:03,209 --> 00:00:05,417
Your cycle on the way to college.

4
00:00:05,817 --> 00:00:06,753
Three trips.

5
00:00:06,753 --> 00:00:09,685
One picture can tell
the whole story of each.

6
00:00:10,135 --> 00:00:11,653
Aarav rides to college.

7
00:00:11,653 --> 00:00:14,135
At the gate, he checks his cycle app.

8
00:00:14,135 --> 00:00:16,385
It shows just one line on a graph.

9
00:00:16,735 --> 00:00:19,103
His friend asks: where did you stop?

10
00:00:19,103 --> 00:00:20,615
Where were you fastest?

11
00:00:20,615 --> 00:00:22,182
Did you ever turn back?

12
00:00:22,682 --> 00:00:24,997
Aarav says one line cannot tell all that.

13
00:00:24,997 --> 00:00:27,795
By the end, you will read
it all from the line.

14
00:00:28,445 --> 00:00:30,451
So what is a position-time graph?

15
00:00:30,801 --> 00:00:33,722
We pick a starting
point, called the origin.

16
00:00:33,722 --> 00:00:36,315
Position x is how far you are from it.

17
00:00:36,315 --> 00:00:37,597
Ahead is positive.

18
00:00:37,997 --> 00:00:40,483
Time goes on the horizontal axis.

19
00:00:40,483 --> 00:00:43,079
Position goes on the vertical axis.

20
00:00:43,479 --> 00:00:47,332
At every moment, we mark one
dot: where you are, at that time.

21
00:00:47,332 --> 00:00:49,846
Join the dots, and you get the x-t graph.

22
00:00:50,446 --> 00:00:53,146
Let's ride with Aarav
on our motion notebook.

23
00:00:53,496 --> 00:00:54,396
Part one.

24
00:00:54,297 --> 00:00:57,218
He rides at a steady
five metres per second.

25
00:00:57,218 --> 00:01:00,177
Every second, he moves
the same five metres.

26
00:01:00,577 --> 00:01:02,429
Equal steps in equal times.

27
00:01:02,429 --> 00:01:05,090
So the graph is a straight sloping line.

28
00:01:05,590 --> 00:01:06,490
Part two.

29
00:01:06,395 --> 00:01:07,965
He stops at a signal.

30
00:01:07,965 --> 00:01:11,694
Time keeps going, but the
position stays at fifty metres.

31
00:01:12,093 --> 00:01:13,573
The graph goes flat.

32
00:01:13,573 --> 00:01:16,345
A flat line means the object is at rest.

33
00:01:16,845 --> 00:01:17,745
Part three.

34
00:01:17,744 --> 00:01:20,402
He rides faster, ten metres per second.

35
00:01:20,402 --> 00:01:22,112
Now the line is steeper.

36
00:01:22,512 --> 00:01:24,285
Steeper line, faster motion.

37
00:01:24,285 --> 00:01:27,038
The steepness of the
line tells you the speed.

38
00:01:27,638 --> 00:01:30,889
Let's turn steepness into a
number, one step at a time.

39
00:01:31,239 --> 00:01:32,139
Step one.

40
00:01:32,032 --> 00:01:33,977
Pick two points on the line.

41
00:01:33,977 --> 00:01:36,358
Read their times and their positions.

42
00:01:37,108 --> 00:01:38,008
Step two.

43
00:01:37,990 --> 00:01:42,155
The change in position, delta
x, is x two minus x one.

44
00:01:42,155 --> 00:01:46,104
The change in time, delta
t, is t two minus t one.

45
00:01:46,854 --> 00:01:47,931
Step three.

46
00:01:47,931 --> 00:01:50,705
Slope equals delta x over delta t.

47
00:01:50,705 --> 00:01:52,636
That is rise over run.

48
00:01:53,386 --> 00:01:54,405
Step four.

49
00:01:54,405 --> 00:01:57,965
Delta x over delta t is
exactly the velocity.

50
00:01:57,965 --> 00:02:01,536
So the slope of an x-t
graph is the velocity.

51
00:02:02,286 --> 00:02:03,334
Remember this.

52
00:02:03,334 --> 00:02:06,703
On a position-time graph,
the slope is the velocity.

53
00:02:07,403 --> 00:02:08,547
Side by side.

54
00:02:08,547 --> 00:02:09,967
One line goes up.

55
00:02:09,967 --> 00:02:13,390
The position grows, so
the velocity is positive.

56
00:02:13,790 --> 00:02:15,548
The other line goes down.

57
00:02:15,548 --> 00:02:16,993
The position shrinks.

58
00:02:16,993 --> 00:02:21,710
The object is coming back towards the
origin, so the velocity is negative.

59
00:02:22,310 --> 00:02:23,393
Now you try.

60
00:02:23,393 --> 00:02:24,907
A line slopes down.

61
00:02:24,907 --> 00:02:28,966
Is the object moving away from
the origin, or towards it?

62
00:02:28,966 --> 00:02:30,312
Pause and decide.

63
00:02:33,512 --> 00:02:34,484
Towards it!

64
00:02:34,484 --> 00:02:39,494
A falling line means x is getting
smaller, so the object moves back towards

65
00:02:39,494 --> 00:02:40,466
the origin.

66
00:02:40,466 --> 00:02:42,305
Its velocity is negative.

67
00:02:42,955 --> 00:02:44,690
Now, what if the line bends?

68
00:02:45,040 --> 00:02:47,096
Here is Aarav's ride from home.

69
00:02:47,096 --> 00:02:51,364
From rest, he speeds up to six
metres per second in four seconds.

70
00:02:51,764 --> 00:02:54,151
Each second he covers more than the last.

71
00:02:54,151 --> 00:02:56,702
So the graph curves
upward, getting steeper.

72
00:02:57,102 --> 00:03:01,658
At any moment, draw a tangent, a line
that just touches the curve there.

73
00:03:01,658 --> 00:03:04,295
Its slope is the velocity at that instant.

74
00:03:04,795 --> 00:03:07,363
Then he cruises at six metres per second.

75
00:03:07,363 --> 00:03:08,499
A straight part.

76
00:03:08,499 --> 00:03:12,961
Then he brakes, and stops in three
seconds, forty five metres from home.

77
00:03:13,561 --> 00:03:14,461
Your turn.

78
00:03:14,388 --> 00:03:17,811
In the last part, the curve
gets flatter and flatter.

79
00:03:17,811 --> 00:03:20,064
Is he speeding up or slowing down?

80
00:03:20,064 --> 00:03:21,281
Pause and decide.

81
00:03:24,481 --> 00:03:25,536
Slowing down!

82
00:03:25,536 --> 00:03:28,834
The tangent gets less
steep, until it is flat.

83
00:03:28,834 --> 00:03:31,274
A flat tangent means zero velocity.

84
00:03:31,274 --> 00:03:32,494
He has stopped.

85
00:03:33,144 --> 00:03:34,879
Now, one worked example.

86
00:03:35,229 --> 00:03:39,846
Aarav's position goes from fifty
metres at twenty seconds to one hundred

87
00:03:39,846 --> 00:03:42,368
and fifty metres at thirty seconds.

88
00:03:42,368 --> 00:03:43,789
Find his velocity.

89
00:03:44,289 --> 00:03:47,671
Delta x is one hundred
and fifty minus fifty.

90
00:03:47,671 --> 00:03:49,785
That is one hundred metres.

91
00:03:50,535 --> 00:03:52,943
Delta t is thirty minus twenty.

92
00:03:52,943 --> 00:03:54,604
That is ten seconds.

93
00:03:55,354 --> 00:03:57,515
Slope is one hundred over ten.

94
00:03:57,515 --> 00:04:00,147
His velocity is ten metres per second.

95
00:04:00,797 --> 00:04:02,263
Now, the exam trap.

96
00:04:02,613 --> 00:04:07,176
Many students think an x-t graph shows
the path of the object, like a hill

97
00:04:07,176 --> 00:04:08,076
it climbs.

98
00:04:08,371 --> 00:04:10,904
But Aarav rides on a flat, straight road.

99
00:04:10,904 --> 00:04:13,314
The graph is not a picture of the road.

100
00:04:13,314 --> 00:04:15,953
It is a record of where he was, and when.

101
00:04:16,603 --> 00:04:18,043
Where is this asked?

102
00:04:18,043 --> 00:04:22,365
As graph reading questions: compare
the slopes of lines, or spot where

103
00:04:22,365 --> 00:04:24,550
the velocity is zero or negative.

104
00:04:25,150 --> 00:04:26,495
Exam level now.

105
00:04:26,495 --> 00:04:28,490
Two cyclists, P and Q.

106
00:04:28,490 --> 00:04:33,665
On the x-t graph, P's line makes
thirty degrees with the time axis, and

107
00:04:33,665 --> 00:04:35,535
Q's makes sixty degrees.

108
00:04:35,535 --> 00:04:38,658
What is the ratio of P's velocity to Q's?

109
00:04:38,658 --> 00:04:40,061
Pause and solve.

110
00:04:45,461 --> 00:04:46,869
The answer is B.

111
00:04:46,869 --> 00:04:48,278
One is to three.

112
00:04:48,778 --> 00:04:53,548
Velocity is the slope, and slope is
tan of the angle with the time axis.

113
00:04:54,048 --> 00:04:56,532
Tan thirty is one over root three.

114
00:04:56,532 --> 00:04:58,366
Tan sixty is root three.

115
00:04:58,866 --> 00:05:02,753
So the ratio is one over root
three, divided by root three.

116
00:05:02,753 --> 00:05:05,451
That is one third, or one is to three.

117
00:05:06,051 --> 00:05:07,511
Here is your recap.

118
00:05:07,511 --> 00:05:09,012
Screenshot this card.

119
00:05:12,212 --> 00:05:15,049
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

120
00:05:15,449 --> 00:05:16,349
One.

121
00:05:15,954 --> 00:05:19,129
What does a flat line on
an x-t graph tell you?

122
00:05:20,029 --> 00:05:20,929
Two.

123
00:05:20,544 --> 00:05:23,723
How do you find the
velocity from an x-t graph?

124
00:05:24,623 --> 00:05:25,523
Three.

125
00:05:25,302 --> 00:05:28,411
Can an x-t graph ever be a vertical line?

126
00:05:28,411 --> 00:05:29,311
Why not?

127
00:05:30,369 --> 00:05:34,315
Next time: what if we plot
velocity instead of position?

128
00:05:34,315 --> 00:05:37,397
The area under that
line hides the distance.

129
00:05:37,397 --> 00:05:39,880
We find out in velocity-time graphs.
